\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces OT Diversity Collapse. (a) OT coupling on a real chromosome detection image (Dataset~2 val set, cropped from a $\sim $46-chromosome metaphase spread). Black rectangles are $8$ GT boxes (labeled with categories); circles above the image in noise space are noise samples $\mathbf  {z}_i$; solid lines = OT (nearest-neighbor) assignment, dashed = random assignment (only $4$ shown to avoid clutter). OT deterministically maps each noise sample to the nearest GT, making the coupling assignment a deterministic function of noise---diversity collapses to zero; random assignment preserves full diversity. (b) Empirical validation of Proposition~\ref {prop:ot-gap}: theoretical $\log K = 3.8427$ vs.\ empirical $\Delta H = 3.8415$ (relative error $0.03\%$).}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ot-theory}{{4}{6}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {V}{\ignorespaces Main ablation on Dataset~2 (independent inference, seed~42 for the first three rows; 3-seed mean for DPM-Solver++, std in text). NFE = total network forward evaluations per image. ``Last-30 std'' is within-run epoch \ensuremath  {\mathrm  {mAP}}{} std. RF dominates the baseline$\to $RF+Heun gain ($+0.077$, $94\%$); solver/step adds only $+0.005$ ($6\%$); Stoch.\ Coupling stabilizes without changing \ensuremath  {\mathrm  {mAP}}{}; DPM-Solver++ matches Heun at $1.71\times $ fewer NFE.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main-ablation}{{V}{6}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{\numberline {\mbox  {IV-B}0b}Dataset~1: RF vs DDPM}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
